{"items":[{"tags":["optimization","deep-learning","pytorch","neural-network"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":78907866,"is_answered":false,"question_id":78906991,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1724458864,"creation_date":1724458864,"body":"I think you are confused about some very basic programming aspects here. Lets step through some things.\nYou create the model:\n\nAt this point you have one single model. Now you create this dict:\n\nAt this point you still have one model. The entries in  all reference the same model state dict. The dict values are all pointers to the exact same object.\nYou create the optimizer dict\n\nAt this point you still have one model. Each dict value is a different optimizer object, but they all reference the same weight objects.\nYou create the model copy dict\n\nNow you have created four new models. You have five models total - four in  plus the original model you cloned from.\nNow your train loop\n\nIf you understand the code you wrote, you can see clearly why this doesn&#39;t work. You use the model in  with the optimizer .  points to one of the model copies you created.  points to the weights of the original model. They are referencing different objects. Loading the state dict via  does not change this because it copies the data values from  into the tensor objects in .\nThe reason you see no change in the weights is because you aren&#39;t updating them. You compute your loss with the weights in , but your optimizer references the weights in the original . There is no connection between the two. If you actually inspect a gradient value of a parameter in the optimizer () you will find it is  because no gradient is being computed. The parameters in the optimizer are unrelated to the parameters you use to calculate your loss.\nI think you are also confused about what you are trying to accomplish. When you say , it implies you want to train separate models on the different inputs. When you say , it implies you want a single model.\nYou can train a single model on all versions of the input, or you can train separate instances of the model on the separate inputs.\n","excerpt":"If you actually inspect a gradient value of a parameter in the optimizer (optimizer[key].<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>[0][&#39;params&#39;][0].grad) you will find it is None because no gradient is being computed. &hellip; ","title":"train an NN model independently for different input versions"},{"tags":["python","pytorch"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":78315870,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1721680297,"creation_date":1712920110,"body":"The task at work is to make a CNN model to do some classification task on images. Also, I should be able to view the feature maps after making a classification on an image i.e. the images obtained after applying convolution or pooling operations. Below is how I defined my CNN class:\n\nThe idea is to store blocks of convolution and pooling layers in the  dictionary with names such as , ,  etc...\nThen, in the forward method, I would run the input images through each block and also store the outputs of each block in the  dictionary to retrieve later ( does this).\nThe problem is that the model&#39;s parameters are not being set properly. Below is the code where I instantiated the model and optimizer:\n\nHowever I ran into the following error:\n\nI printed out the parameter list and it is empty. I don&#39;t understand why this is happening.\nIs my way of defining the architecture or forward method wrong and PyTorch expects some specific behavior when subclassing ? If so what is it and how can I change my class? Any extra information on how Pytorch actually stores the class parameters is most welcomed.\n","excerpt":"= list(params)\n    272         if len(<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>) == 0:\n--&gt; 273             raise ValueError(&quot;optimizer got an empty parameter list&quot;)\n    274         if not isinstance(<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>[0], dict):\n    275 &hellip; <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> = [{&#39;params&#39;: <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>}]\n\nValueError: optimizer got an empty parameter list\n\nI printed out the parameter list and it is empty. &hellip; ","title":"pytorch CNN model parameters are not storing properly"},{"tags":["machine-learning","pytorch","hook"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":78420856,"is_answered":false,"question_id":78392429,"item_type":"answer","score":-1,"last_activity_date":1714671406,"creation_date":1714671406,"body":"Probably the easiest way to do this is to dump the param groups and state dict of the optimizer after every  call. This will let you capture the parameters, gradients and optimizer state.\nThe code below shows an example with a simple MLP, but the  function should work for all models so long as the param groups/state dict don&#39;t have multiple levels of nesting.\n\nThe code logs after  to grab gradients. You could add additional logging after , but the values updated after  (params, optimizer averages, etc) will be captured on the next iteration. You can also use values from one iteration to recreate the next. For example, with Adam:\n\n","excerpt":"= &#39;params&#39;}\n        <span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span>[&#39;params&#39;] = []\n        <span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span>[&#39;param_grads&#39;] = []\n        for param in group[&#39;params&#39;]:\n            <span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span>[&#39;params&#39;].append(param.data.cpu().clone()) # move tensor &hellip; <span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span>[&#39;param_grads&#39;].append(None)\n\n        param_groups.append(<span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span>)\n\n    output[&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>&#39;] = <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>\n    \n    return output\n\n\n\nnet = MLP(64, 20, 10)\n\nopt = torch.optim.Adam(net.parameters &hellip; ","title":"How can we capture update done with optimizer.step() in an elegant way?"},{"tags":["pytorch","conv-neural-network","image-classification"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":77291395,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1712109169,"creation_date":1697249984,"body":"(EDIT: Added the whole code)I am trying to run the following:\n\nMy first run was successful because  was disabled. Now that I enabled it I am getting the following error:  and . From what I understand, during the transform.ToTensor() the input images (3channels,224H,224W) are being converted to Pytorch tensors.  is trying to normalize a 4D tensor but is receiving a 3D one. How would I alter transforms so the tensors are 4D (in batches)? I read about  but all the examples are on single tensors.\n","excerpt":"for epoch in range(EPOCHS):\n    print(&quot;EPOCH:&quot;, epoch)\n    train(model, device, train_loader, optimizer, epoch)\n    scheduler.step()\n    print(&#39;current Learning Rate: &#39;, optimizer.state_dict()[&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; ","title":"How to batch transform tensors in a cnn?"},{"tags":["c++","pytorch","libtorch"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":70674717,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1710087002,"creation_date":1641944550,"body":"I need to create an instance of . I looked through the  code as an example, and accordingly copied the following code from there. But, with the provided headers, it still does not recognize .\nI appreciate any help or comment on this matter.\n\n","excerpt":"include &lt;torch/nn/module.h&gt;\n#include &lt;torch/serialize/archive.h&gt;\n#include &lt;torch/utils.h&gt;\n\n#include &lt;ATen/ATen.h&gt;\n\nvoid get_state(torch::optim::Optimizer *optimizer){\n    for (auto&amp; group : optimizer-&gt;<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; ","title":"Create an instance of AdamParamState"},{"tags":["python","deep-learning","pytorch","resnet"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":77329798,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1698043535,"creation_date":1697794200,"body":"I&#39;m implementing a SimCLR/ResNet18 architecture over a custom dataset.\nI know that\n\nNumber of Iterations in One Epoch=Batch Size/Total Training Dataset Size​\n\nAnd if the result is floating then the size of the last batch is adapted for the leftovers (the &#39;fractional batch&#39;).\nHowever in my case, this last mechanism does not seem to work.\nMy dataset is of size 7000.\nIf I give a batch size of 100, I then have 7000/70=100 iterations, without fractional batch and the training goes on.\nHowever, if I give a batch size of 32 for instance, then I have the following error (full stack trace)\n\nHere is some code (error happens at last line):\n\nand here are my classes:\n\nFor records, my dataset has 7000 RGB images of size 224x224.\nHow come my last &#39;fractional&#39; batch is not supported ?\nMany thanks for your help.\n","excerpt":"self.log(&#39;Contrastive loss&#39;, loss, on_step=True, on_epoch=True, prog_bar=True, logger=True)\n        return loss\n\n    def configure_optimizers(self):\n        max_epochs = int(self.config.epochs)\n        <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; = define_param_groups(self.model, self.config.weight_decay, &#39;adam&#39;)\n        lr = self.config.lr\n        optimizer = Adam(<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>, lr=lr, weight_decay=self.config.weight_decay)\n\n        print(f&#39;Optimizer &hellip; ","title":"SimCLR/ResNet18 : last fractional batch mecanism not functional ? (tensor shapes incompatible)"},{"tags":["python","pytorch","conv-neural-network","resnet","torchvision"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":76693733,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1689503246,"creation_date":1689423962,"body":"I am trying to create a custom optimiser which extends the SGD optimiser.\nThis was my first attempt (at the initialisation function):\n\nWith this, the test accuracy of a ResNet on CIFAR-10 comes to be about 88%.\nFor reference, if I call  on a really simple toy network, the relevant attributes are:\n\nHowever, if I change the initialisation function as follows, then the performance is consistently better, with test accuracy of around 95%:\n\nIf I call  on the same example network:\n\nI am struggling to understand this discrepancy in performance, because the relevant attributes (, , etc. printed above) are identical (except for the order of key-value pairs in the dictionaries, with  appearing at the end with  and towards the start with ).\nCan anyone provide any insight?\n","excerpt":": 0, &#39;nesterov&#39;: False, &#39;maximize&#39;: False, &#39;foreach&#39;: None}]\n------------------------------------\n\nI am struggling to understand this discrepancy in performance, because the relevant attributes (<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; ","title":"Initialisation function for custom optimiser in PyTorch affects neural network performance?"},{"tags":["tensorflow","huggingface-transformers"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":76269613,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1684309216,"creation_date":1684309216,"body":"I need to inject some warm up steps to replicate results from a paper. I&#39;m currently using HuggingFace&#39;s TF implementation and the code I&#39;m working on isn&#39;t using a Trainer. The only sample implementations I&#39;ve found so far are using Trainers and on PyTorch. I&#39;ve found WarmUp but there is no information on how and where to use it.\nHere&#39;s the base code I&#39;m using:\n\nI tried following this to create a scheduler and followed the advice here to pass the optimizer and the scheduler as a tuple in the optimizer argument:\n\nbut I just get an error of\n\n&#39;AdamW&#39; object has no attribute &#39;param_groups&#39;\n\nso I believe that&#39;s not the right way to do it. Any pointers to the right direction would be very useful.\n","excerpt":"scheduler,\n                loss = SparseCategoricalCrossentropy(from_logits=True),\n                metrics = [&quot;accuracy&quot;]\n                )\n\nbut I just get an error of\n\n&#39;AdamW&#39; object has no attribute &#39;<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; ","title":"Warm up on HuggingFace Tensorflow implementation without using Trainer"},{"tags":["python","deep-learning","pytorch","distributed-system"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":76027752,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1681702602,"creation_date":1681647997,"body":"I use DDP of pytorch to train my model with two RTX3090 GPUs on a same machine, and I found the time cost is over than training without DDP on a single GPU. It costs about 133s to train my model with DDP while it costs 105s to train it without DDP per epoch. The data is correctly loaded into memory of the two GPUs. It seems that when using PyTorch&#39;s DistributedDataParallel (DDP) for training, the two GPUs did not perform parallel computing. Instead, the same task was computed twice.\nMy main code is as below:\n\nI use the following command to execute my main code:\n\nSome output log during training is as below\n\nDoes anyone know how to solve this issue, thanks!\n","excerpt":"logger.write(&#39;overall: %.4f,  open: %.4f,  close: %.4f,   loss: %.4f,   lr: %.6f, time: %.4f&#39;\n                         % (score, open_score, close_score, total_loss, optimizer.state_dict()[&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; ","title":"Time cost of training with pytorch DDP with multi-GPUs"},{"tags":["python","machine-learning","pytorch","scheduler"],"question_score":3,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":4,"is_answered":true,"question_id":72058575,"item_type":"question","score":3,"last_activity_date":1677031895,"creation_date":1651238146,"body":"In PyTorch I have configured SGD like this:\n\nMy requirements are:\n\nTotal epochs are 100\nEvery 30 epochs learning rate is decreased by a factor of 10\nDecreasing learning rate will stop at 60 epochs\n\nSo for 100 epochs I will get two times a decrease of  of my learning rate.\nI read about learning rate scheduler, available in  so I decided to try using that instead of manually adjusting the learning rate:\n\nHowever I am getting\n\nI read a post here and I still don&#39;t get how I would use the scheduler for my scenario. Maybe I am just not understanding the definition of  given that the documentation is very brief on this parameter:\n\nlast_epoch (int) – The index of last epoch. Default: .\n\nSince the argument is made available to the user and there is no explicit prohibition on using a scheduler for less epochs than the optimizer itself, I am starting to think it&#39;s a bug.\n","excerpt":"File &quot;D:\\env\\test\\lib\\site-packages\\torch\\optim\\lr_scheduler.py&quot;, line 39, in __init__\n    raise KeyError(&quot;param &#39;initial_lr&#39; is not specified &quot;\nKeyError: &quot;param &#39;initial_lr&#39; is not specified in <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; ","title":"How to fix &quot;initial_lr not specified when resuming optimizer&quot; error for scheduler?"},{"tags":["python","pytorch","neural-network"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":75291673,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1675125800,"creation_date":1675125800,"body":"I&#39;m trying to copy the DeepAR method from the pytorch_forecasting documentation and I keep getting this problem with this piece of code.\n\nBut I get this bug\n\n","excerpt":", self.root_device)\n\nFile ~\\anaconda3\\envs\\pytorch_forecasting\\lib\\site-packages\\torch\\optim\\optimizer.py:244, in Optimizer.load_state_dict(self, state_dict)\n    241     return new_group\n    242 <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; __setstate__({&#39;state&#39;: state, &#39;<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>&#39;: <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>})\n\nFile ~\\anaconda3\\envs\\pytorch_forecasting\\lib\\site-packages\\pytorch_forecasting\\optim.py:133, in Ranger. &hellip; ","title":"PyTorch Forecasting / PyTorch Lightning learning rate find function key error problem"},{"tags":["pytorch-lightning"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":74553742,"item_type":"question","score":2,"last_activity_date":1669294375,"creation_date":1669243375,"body":"I would like to manually configure the learning rate scheduling using  in the following way:\n\nWhat I have tried is the following:\n\nbut I get back the following\n\nEDIT:\nI&#39;ve simply found the solution by doing:\n\nMy last perplexity is: is this something I should add in some Callback instead of leaving it directly into the training step?\n","excerpt":"following\nTypeError: &#39;LightningAdamW&#39; object is not subscriptable\n\nEDIT:\nI&#39;ve simply found the solution by doing:\n    if self.current_epoch % 200 == 0 :\n        \n        for g in self.optimizers().<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; ","title":"Manually configuring a scheduling for learning rate in `pytorch_lightning`"},{"tags":["pytorch","learning-rate"],"question_score":9,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":73629646,"is_answered":false,"question_id":73629330,"item_type":"answer","score":20,"last_activity_date":1662520744,"creation_date":1662519938,"body":"Updates to model parameters are handled by an optimizer in PyTorch. When you define the optimizer you have the option of partitioning the model parameters into different groups, called param groups. Each param group can have different optimizer settings. For example one group of parameters could have learning rate of 0.1 and another could have learning rate of 0.01.\nTo do what you&#39;re asking, you can just make every parameter belong to a different param group. You&#39;ll need some way to keep track of which param group corresponds to which parameter. Once you&#39;ve defined the optimizer with different groups you can update the learning rate whenever you want, including at training time.\nFor example, say we have the following simple linear model\n\nand suppose we want learning rates for each trainable parameter initialized according to the following:\n\nWe can use this dictionary to define a different learning rate for each parameter when we initialize the optimizer.\n\nAlternatively, we could omit the  entry and each param group would be initialized with the default learning rate ( in this case).\nAt training time if we wanted to update the learning rates we could do so by iterating over each of the  and updating the  entry for each of them. For example, in the following simplified training loop, we update the learning rates before each step.\n\nwhich prints\n\n","excerpt":".\n# Build <span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span> where each group consists of a single parameter.\n# `param_group_names` is created so we can keep track of which <span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span>\n# corresponds to which parameter.\n<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> = []\nparam_group_names &hellip; in zip(param_group_names, optimizer.param_groups):\n        <span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span>[&#39;lr&#39;] = learning_rates[name] / (i + 1)\n        print(f&#39;    {name}: {<span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span>[&quot;lr&quot;]}&#39;)\n\n    optimizer.step()\n\nwhich prints\nstep &hellip; ","title":"What exactly is meant by param_groups in pytorch?"},{"tags":["pytorch","learning-rate"],"question_score":9,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":73629330,"item_type":"question","score":9,"last_activity_date":1662520744,"creation_date":1662516136,"body":"I would like to update learning rates corresponding to each weight matrix and each bias in pytorch during training. The answers here and here and many other answers I found online talk about doing this using the model&#39;s  which to the best of my knowledge applies learning rates in groups, not layer weight/bias specific. I also want to update the learning rates during training, not pre-setting them with .\nAny help is appreciated.\n","excerpt":"The answers here and here and many other answers I found online talk about doing this using the model&#39;s <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> which to the best of my knowledge applies learning rates in groups, not layer weight &hellip; ","title":"What exactly is meant by param_groups in pytorch?"},{"tags":["pytorch","learning-rate","viewmodel-savedstate"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":true,"answer_id":72593480,"is_answered":false,"question_id":72589798,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1655047033,"creation_date":1655047033,"body":"You can change the learning rate of your optimizer by accessing its  attribute. Depending on whether you have multiple groups or not, you can do the following (after having loaded the checkpoint onto it):\n\n","excerpt":"You can change the learning rate of your optimizer by accessing its <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> attribute. &hellip; ","title":"Re-setting learning rate while training in Pytorch"},{"tags":["python","deep-learning","pytorch"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":72545656,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1654692753,"creation_date":1654691055,"body":"Am new in the deep learning concept using pytorch and am try to build a binary classifier model. I have tried some of the solution here on stack overflow but I can&#39;t seem to solve it. Maybe it is due to the nature of my code. Can someone figure out what could be the cause of this error in my code\nHere is my code\n\nI get the error\n\nThe error is mainly generated from this section of code\n\nWhat could be the cause in my code.\nHere is the full stack trade\n\n","excerpt":"__init__(self, params, defaults)\n     47 <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> = list(params)\n     48 if len(<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>) == 0:\n---&gt; 49     raise ValueError(&quot;optimizer got an empty parameter list&quot;)\n     50 if not isinstance(<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; [0], dict):\n     51     <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> = [{&#39;params&#39;: <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>}]\n\nValueError: optimizer got an empty parameter list &hellip; ","title":"Pytorch ;Optimizer got an empty parameter list"},{"tags":["python","pytorch"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":72510086,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1654511949,"creation_date":1654453670,"body":"I&#39;m trying to use a U-net to apply image segmentation to the RUGD dataset.  I get the following error, I&#39;m stuck.\n\nInstalling segmentation-models for pytorch\n\nInstalling Libraries\n\nName and direct paths\n\nCreate dataframe\n\nTotal Images:  7387\nSplit Test and Train\n\nImage size (550, 688, 3)\nMask size (550, 688, 3)\nimage rendered\nDefine U-net\n\nApply augmentation and initiate dataloader\n\nbuild the model\n\nrender the model\n\nDefine Miou\n\ndef get_lr(optimizer):\nfor param_group in optimizer.param_groups:\nreturn param_group[&#39;lr&#39;]\n\nThis the point where the model fails\n\nRemainder of the code\n\n","excerpt":"item()\n                \n                iou = (intersect + smooth) / (union + smooth)\n                iou_per_class.append(iou)\n        return np.nanmean(iou_per_class)\n\ndef get_lr(optimizer):\nfor <span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span> &hellip; in optimizer.param_groups:\nreturn <span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span>[&#39;lr&#39;]\ndef fit(epochs, model, train_loader, val_loader, criterion, optimizer, scheduler, patch=False):\n    torch.cuda.empty_cache()\n    train_losses = [] &hellip; ","title":"Implementing a U-net for image segmentation and have the following error: NameError: name &#39;val_iou&#39; is not defined"},{"tags":["pytorch","pytorch-lightning"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":71130211,"is_answered":false,"question_id":71130015,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1644943412,"creation_date":1644943412,"body":"In the library code, I found:\n\nWhen commenting this in, everything works normally.\nI leave the question open because it does not feel like a good solution to change the underlying library or to just copy this code section to my file.\n","excerpt":"In the library code, I found:\n# if not isinstance(<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>[0], dict):\n#             <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> = [{&#39;params&#39;: <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>}]\n\nWhen commenting this in, everything works normally. &hellip; ","title":"Why does pytorch lightnings configure_optimizer throw AssertionError: param group must be a dict?"},{"tags":["python","machine-learning","optimization","pytorch"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":70960358,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1643827354,"creation_date":1643824009,"body":"Quick overview of my issue: I&#39;m implementing a custom optimizer, and all I want to do is add two class variables and an extra instruction to the  function. Here&#39;s the class code for reference:\n\nI don&#39;t really understand what the  are. I know that when the  are passed to an optimizer like\n\nthey are taken and converted into the  as a class variable, but I don&#39;t know a simple way to just get the original params out as they were. Is there a way to extract those original  that&#39;s already implemented in the base optimizer class? My goal is to not have to use that nested for loop because I&#39;m planning on extending other torch optimizers in the same manner and for readability I&#39;d rather avoid writing more code than I need to.\n","excerpt":"privacy.noise_and_clip_parameters(\n            params,\n            l2_norm_clip=self.l2_norm_clip,\n            noise_multiplier=self.noise_multiplier,\n        )\n        return closure\n\nI don&#39;t really understand what the <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; I know that when the model.parameters() are passed to an optimizer like\noptimizer = torch.optim.SGD(model.parameters(), lr=0.001, momentum=0.9)\n\nthey are taken and converted into the <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> as a &hellip; ","title":"How to get the params in a custom implementation of a pytorch optimizer?"},{"tags":["c++","pytorch","torch","libtorch"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":70710538,"item_type":"question","score":1,"last_activity_date":1642163094,"creation_date":1642163094,"body":"I have an tensor with type , and I need to get its value as a regular  object. I know how to convert a  object with a regular type to a  object, but the same procedure does not work for tensors with type . I found an expensive way to do that, it needs to iterate over every single parameter on the  object which can be very expensive.\nI faced these tensors with type  in the state of the optimizer, where I need to get the state value of different optimizer and modify them based on a given algorithm.\nBelow is the piece of the code which gets the tensors of .\nI appreciate any comment on this.\n\n","excerpt":"void get_state(torch::optim::Optimizer *optimizer){\n    for (auto&amp; group : optimizer-&gt;<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>()) {\n        for (auto &amp;p : group.params()) {\n            if (! &hellip; ","title":"Convert a tensor with type caffe2::TypeMeta to std::vector"},{"tags":["wordpress","wordpress-theming","visual-composer"],"question_score":4,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":45228985,"item_type":"question","score":4,"last_activity_date":1640807789,"creation_date":1500611324,"body":"Is there an option when using param_group to have new rows toggled open by default. It seems odd that when using a param_group, you add a new row row, and its toggled collapsed. It would be great if when you add new rows, they&#39;re open by default.\n","excerpt":"Is there an option when using <span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span> to have new rows toggled open by default. It seems odd that when using a <span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span>, you add a new row row, and its toggled collapsed. &hellip; ","title":"Visual Composer param_group open by default when adding new rows?"},{"tags":["python","machine-learning","pytorch","autoencoder"],"question_score":3,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":69234232,"item_type":"question","score":3,"last_activity_date":1631965619,"creation_date":1631964684,"body":"I have this Variational autoencoder and I want to use Adam for its optimizer but it has this error I don&#39;t know what is wrong here\n\nand here is the error\n\nhow I can solve this?\n","excerpt":"__init__(params, defaults)\n File &quot;C:\\Users\\khashayar\\anaconda3\\envs\\deeplearning\\lib\\site-packages\\torch\\optim\\optimizer.py&quot;, line 47, in __init__\n   <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> = list(params)\nTypeError: &#39;method&#39; object &hellip; ","title":"How can I solve the problem that method is not iterable?"},{"tags":["ruby-on-rails-4","apipie"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":40801433,"is_answered":false,"question_id":35821499,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1629021196,"creation_date":1480065699,"body":"I know that it&#39;s a little bit late to reply, but here&#39;s how to do it:\n\nCreate a controller that extends ApiPie:\n\n\n\nDefine  in that controller:\n\n\n\nExtend this controller from your second controller:\n\n\n\nCall the  before your function using:\n\n\n","excerpt":"I know that it&#39;s a little bit late to reply, but here&#39;s how to do it:\n\nCreate a controller that extends ApiPie:\n\nclass ApiController &lt; ActionController::Base extend Apipie::DSL::Concern\n\n\nDefine <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; before your function using:\n\n<span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span> :param_errors, ApiController\ndef my_method\n    # ...\nend &hellip; ","title":"Combine params.required with apipie param_group"},{"tags":["ruby-on-rails-4","apipie"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":false,"question_id":35821499,"item_type":"question","score":2,"last_activity_date":1629021196,"creation_date":1457223010,"body":"How can I combine Rails&#39;  with apiepie&#39;s  to avoid duplication?\n","excerpt":"How can I combine Rails&#39; params.require with apiepie&#39;s <span class=\"highlight\">param_group</span> to avoid duplication? &hellip; ","title":"Combine params.required with apipie param_group"},{"tags":["python","pytorch","huggingface-transformers"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":68525174,"is_answered":false,"question_id":68523070,"item_type":"answer","score":1,"last_activity_date":1627325732,"creation_date":1627279568,"body":"You can create a custom scheduler by just creating a function in a class that takes in an optimizer and its state dicts and edits the values in its param_groups.\nTo understand how to structure this in a class, just take a look at how Pytorch creates its schedulers and use the same functions just change the functionality to your liking.\nThe Permalink I found that will be a good reference is over here\nEDIT After comments:\nThis is like a template you can use\n\nYou can add more functions for increased functionality. Or you can just use a function to update your learning rate. This will take in the optimizer and change it optimizer.param_groups[0][&quot;lr&quot;] and return the new optimizer.\n","excerpt":"You can create a custom scheduler by just creating a function in a class that takes in an optimizer and its state dicts and edits the values in its <span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>. &hellip; ","title":"Difference between transformers schedulers and Pytorch schedulers"},{"tags":["python","serialization","deep-learning","pytorch","tensor"],"question_score":401,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":55734349,"is_answered":false,"question_id":42703500,"item_type":"answer","score":36,"last_activity_date":1626986771,"creation_date":1555527663,"body":"The pickle Python library implements binary protocols for serializing and de-serializing a Python object.\nWhen you  (or when you use PyTorch) it will  for you and you don&#39;t need to call  and  directly, which are the methods to save and to load the object.\nIn fact,  and  will wrap  and  for you.\nA  the other answer mentioned deserves just a few more notes.\nWhat  do we have inside PyTorch?\nThere are actually two s.\nThe PyTorch model is  which has  call to get learnable parameters (w and b).\nThese learnable parameters, once randomly set, will update over time as we learn.\nLearnable parameters are the first .\nThe second  is the optimizer state dict. You recall that the optimizer is used to improve our learnable parameters. But the optimizer  is fixed. Nothing to learn there.\nBecause  objects are Python dictionaries, they can be easily saved, updated, altered, and restored, adding a great deal of modularity to PyTorch models and optimizers.\nLet&#39;s create a super simple model to explain this:\n\nThis code will output the following:\n\nNote this is a minimal model. You may try to add stack of sequential\n\nNote that only layers with learnable parameters (convolutional layers, linear layers, etc.) and registered buffers (batchnorm layers) have entries in the model&#39;s .\nNon-learnable things belong to the optimizer object , which contains information about the optimizer&#39;s state, as well as the hyperparameters used.\nThe rest of the story is the same; in the inference phase (this is a phase when we use the model after training) for predicting; we do predict based on the parameters we learned. So for the inference, we just need to save the parameters .\n\nAnd to use later\nmodel.load_state_dict(torch.load(filepath))\nmodel.eval()\nNote: Don&#39;t forget the last line  this is crucial after loading the model.\nAlso don&#39;t try to save . The  is just the generator object.\nOn the other hand,  saves the model object itself, but keep in mind the model doesn&#39;t have the optimizer&#39;s . Check the other excellent answer by @Jadiel de Armas to save the optimizer&#39;s state dict.\n","excerpt":"[ 0.0479,  0.1760,  0.1712,  0.2244,  0.1408]], requires_grad=True)\nModel bias:\nParameter containing:\ntensor([ 0.4112, -0.0733], requires_grad=True)\n---\nOptimizer&#39;s state_dict:\nstate      {}\n<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; ","title":"How do I save a trained model in PyTorch?"},{"tags":["python","deep-learning","pytorch","reinforcement-learning"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":68442914,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1626711495,"creation_date":1626707645,"body":"While I use torch.optim.Adam and exponential decay_lr in my PPO algorithm:\n\nThe initial lr=0.1, and GAMMA=0.9.\nThen I print the lr in my epoch dynamiclly with:\n\nBut, something wrong with this, and the bug is:\n\nThen, if I delete the &quot;print()&quot; sentence, it does work well!\nSo, it&#39;s bothering me very much.\n","excerpt":"if time_step % update_timestep == 0:\n                ppo_agent.update()\n                print(f&#39;__________start update_______________&#39;)\n                print(ppo_agent.optimizer.state_dict()[&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; ","title":"How to print the adjusting learning rate in Pytorch?"},{"tags":["python","pytorch"],"question_score":0,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":false,"answer_count":0,"is_answered":false,"question_id":67586756,"item_type":"question","score":0,"last_activity_date":1621342910,"creation_date":1621342910,"body":"Im trying to load a checkpoint in pytorch neural network and step down the learning rate with scheduler but it&#39;s not working and line #1 and line #2 print the same thing. sometimes it gives an error like this  and Im using this code at the end of my training loop in an if statement. if further informations you think is necessary please make a comment. cause I think it&#39;s enough right now.\n\n","excerpt":"model_state&#39;])\nmodel.to(device)\nmodel.eval()\n\noptimizer = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=learning_rate)\noptimizer.load_state_dict(checkpoint[&#39;optim_state&#39;])\n\nprint(&#39;lr&#39;,optimizer.state_dict()[&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; &#39;][0][&#39;lr&#39;]) #1\nlosss=.9\nscheduler.step(losss)\nprint(&#39;&lt;&lt;&lt;back to the best&gt;&gt;&gt;&#39;)\nprint(&#39;lr&#39;,optimizer.state_dict()[&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span>&#39;][0][&#39;lr&#39;]) #2 &hellip; ","title":"pytorch nn loading checkpoint and steping scheduler"},{"tags":["python","machine-learning","scikit-learn","pytorch","mnist"],"question_score":1,"is_accepted":false,"answer_id":67248363,"is_answered":false,"question_id":67240725,"item_type":"answer","score":0,"last_activity_date":1619355321,"creation_date":1619306861,"body":"\nSplit training data into 2 part for validation and training.\n\nobtain training indices that will be used for validation\n\ndefine samplers for obtaining training and validation batches\n\ncombine dataset and sampler to prepare dataloader.\n\n2.Use validation data for validate the model.After each epoch training loss and validation loss will be printed , and only parameters with minimum validation loss will be save.\n\n","excerpt":"valid_losses))\n        # save current model\n        torch.save(model.state_dict(), &#39;model_cifer.pt&#39;)\n        valid_loss_min = valid_losses\n    print(&#39;Learning rate: {:.5f}&#39;.format(optimizer.state_dict()[&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; ","title":"Can the verification performance be output from MNIST training data and test data?"},{"tags":["loops","validation","neural-network","pytorch","nan"],"question_score":2,"is_accepted":false,"has_accepted_answer":true,"answer_count":1,"is_answered":true,"question_id":67173932,"item_type":"question","score":2,"last_activity_date":1618985563,"creation_date":1618901837,"body":"Hey so I am trying my hand at image classification/transfer learning using the monkey species dataset and the resnet50 with a modified final fc layer to predict just the 10 classes. Eveything is working until I use model.train() and model.eval() then after the first epoch it starts to return nans and the accuracy drops off as you&#39;ll see below. I&#39;m curious why is this only when switching to train/eval....?\nFirst I import the model and attach the classifier and freeze the parameters\n\nThen setting my loss func, optimizer, and shceduler\n\nThen setting the training and validation loops\n\nThe model starts to train but then slowly becomes nan values\n\n","excerpt":"torch.tensor(val_losses).mean()\n  # Step the scheduler for the next epoch\n  scheduler.step()\n  # Print the updated learning rate\n  print(&#39;Learning Rate Set To: {:.5f}&#39;.format(optimizer.state_dict()[&#39;<span class=\"highlight\">param_groups</span> &hellip; ","title":"model.train() and model.eval() causing nan values"}],"has_more":true,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":243}